<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulysses S. Grant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Ulysses</a> S. Grant was an American general and the 18th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. As commander of the Union armies he defeated the Confederacy in the Civil War of 1861–1865, and as president from 1869 to 1877 he oversaw Reconstruction in the South. During a world tour in 1879 he offered his good offices to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in their dispute over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant.jpg" alt="Ulysses S. Grant" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brady-Handy Photograph Collection, Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulysses_S._Grant_1870-1880_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some officials fled to Fujian to seek Qing support, but negotiations, in which the former American president <b>Ulysses S. Grant</b> offered his good offices, came to nothing, and the islands remained Japanese.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert E. Lee surrendered the Army of Northern Virginia to <b>Ulysses S. Grant</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ulysses S. Grant</b> pulled his Union troops from their position at Cold Harbor, Virginia, and moved south, leaving the battle to the Confederate army of Robert E. Lee.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>President <b>Ulysses S. Grant</b> signed the act setting aside Yellowstone's geysers and canyons 'for the benefit and enjoyment of the people'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Union troops under Brigadier General <b>Ulysses S. Grant</b> overran a Confederate camp at Belmont, Missouri, but were forced to retreat when Confederate reinforcements arrived.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Point Pleasant, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Wilton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.89,-84.23&amp;year=1862" aria-label="Point Pleasant on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2462 769 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ulysses S. Grant"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2912 1039h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2912 1039h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.89,-84.23&amp;year=1862">What was here in 1862? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No terms except an unconditional and immediate surrender can be accepted. I propose to move immediately upon your works.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to General Buckner at Fort Donelson, 16 February 1862 &#183; original &#183; as printed in his Personal Memoirs (1885) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For myself, I was bitterly opposed to the measure, and to this day regard the war, which resulted, as one of the most unjust ever waged by a stronger against a weaker nation.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), on the war with Mexico &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It was an instance of a republic following the bad example of European monarchies, in not considering justice in their desire to acquire additional territory.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), on the war with Mexico &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I felt like anything rather than rejoicing at the downfall of a foe who had fought so long and valiantly, and had suffered so much for a cause, though that cause was, I believe, one of the worst for which a people ever fought…&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), on the surrender at Appomattox &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I feel that we are on the eve of a new era, when there is to be great harmony between the Federal and Confederate.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), conclusion, written as he was dying &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am not aware of ever having used a profane expletive in my life; but I would have the charity to excuse those who may have done so, if they were in charge of a train of Mexican pack mules at the time.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), on the war with Mexico &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
